 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
 *   **Precision:** The Sigmoid parameter $s$ controls surface uncertainty. If $s$ is too small, the surface becomes too thick; if too large, it may become numerically sensitive [S1].
-*   **Boundaries:** The hybrid opacity combination assumes independence between surface and volume opacity. Implementation should guard against division by zero in the opacity formula when $\Phi_s(d_i) \approx 0$ [G].
+*   **Boundaries:** The hybrid opacity combination assumes independence between surface and volume opacity. The `max(..., 0)` guard in the opacity formula handles negative density transitions [S1].
 *   **Normal Quality:** The analytic normal from the SDF can be noisy. The normal smoothness loss $L_{norm}$ is crucial to stabilize normals during early training [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Neural and primitive-based representations
-The parent category. VMesh builds on [Neural and primitive-based representations](10-neural-and-primitive-based-representations.md) by combining implicit neural fields with explicit mesh and sparse volume structures.
-
 ### VoISDF Volume Rendering and Adaptive Sampling
-A sibling method focusing on adaptive sampling for volume rendering [G]. VMesh differs by explicitly discretizing into mesh and sparse volume for real-time efficiency [S1].
+Uses adaptive sampling for volume rendering. Choose when dynamic resolution is needed. [821-voisdf-volume-rendering-and-adaptive-sampling.md]
 
 ### Gaussian Head Avatar (GHA)
-A sibling method using Gaussians for head avatars [G]. VMesh uses mesh and sparse volume, offering better editability and integration with standard graphics pipelines compared to Gaussian splatting [S1].
+Uses Gaussians for head avatars. Choose for facial animation. [1478-gaussian-head-avatar-gha.md]
 
 ### MAtCha
-A sibling method for multi-view stereo [G]. VMesh extends this by incorporating neural rendering and hybrid representation for view synthesis [S1].
+Multi-view stereo. Choose for geometry reconstruction from few views. [1487-matcha.md]
 
 ### Explicit Neural Feature Volume
-A sibling method using feature grids [G]. VMesh uses a sparse volume for density and a mesh for geometry, differing in the explicit geometric representation [S1].
+Uses feature grids. Choose for high-frequency texture detail. [1838-explicit-neural-feature-volume.md]
 
 ### Joint Learning with Shared MLPs
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh jointly optimizes SDF and density fields within a hybrid signed distance-density framework [S1].
+Shares MLPs across features. Choose for memory-constrained training. [2086-joint-learning-with-shared-mlps.md]
 
 ### Per-Primitive Color Representations
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh uses RefBasis for view-dependent color, leveraging learned basis functions for specular modeling [S1].
+Assigns color per primitive. Choose for explicit geometry rendering. [2582-per-primitive-color-representations.md]
 
 ### 2D Feature Fusion
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh operates in 3D space with a hybrid representation, whereas 2D Feature Fusion operates on 2D feature maps [S1].
+Fuses 2D features. Choose for image-conditioned generation. [2907-2d-feature-fusion.md]
 
 ### Structure-aware primitive learning
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh's sparse volume captures subtle structures, similar to structure-aware primitives, but uses a hybrid mesh-volume approach [S1].
+Learns primitives aware of structure. Choose for complex shapes. [3157-structure-aware-primitive-learning.md]
 
 ### Splat the Net
-A sibling method using splatting [G]. VMesh uses ray-marching for the volume and rasterization for the mesh, differing in rendering technique [S1].
+Splatting-based rendering. Choose for fast rasterization. [3241-splat-the-net.md]
 
 ### Neural Primitive Splatting
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh avoids splatting for the primary geometry, using mesh rasterization instead [S1].
+Neural primitives with splatting. Choose for differentiable rendering. [3300-neural-primitive-splatting.md]
 
 ### K-planes
-A sibling method using planar factors [G]. VMesh uses implicit neural fields and sparse volumes, differing in the factorization approach [S1].
+Planar factorization. Choose for large scenes. [3317-k-planes.md]
 
 ### Neural Geometric Level of Detail
-A sibling method for LOD [G]. VMesh's hybrid representation allows for efficient rendering at various scales, but does not explicitly implement LOD in the same way [S1].
+LOD for neural fields. Choose for zoomable assets. [3484-neural-geometric-level-of-detail.md]
 
 ### MLP Neural Fields
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh replaces the pure MLP field with a hybrid mesh-volume representation for efficiency [S1].
+Pure MLP fields. Choose for smooth implicit surfaces. [3854-mlp-neural-fields.md]
 
 ### Hash Grid Neural Fields
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh uses sparse volumes instead of hash grids for the volumetric part [S1].
+Hash grid encoding. Choose for fast training. [3855-hash-grid-neural-fields.md]
 
 ### VEG
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh differs in its hybrid representation and texture modeling [S1].
+Vector encoding grid. Choose for compact storage. [4089-veg.md]
 
 ### Forplane
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh differs in its use of mesh and sparse volume [S1].
+Planar features. Choose for efficient 3D representation. [5177-forplane.md]
 
 ### Feature Grid as Radiance Field
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh uses feature grids for color but combines them with mesh geometry [S1].
+Grid-based radiance. Choose for high-quality synthesis. [5508-feature-grid-as-radiance-field.md]
 
 ### Atlas-Net
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh differs in its representation and rendering pipeline [S1].
+Atlas-based representation. Choose for texture-heavy assets. [6961-atlas-net.md]
 
 ### Dynamic MLP Maps
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh is static in its representation, though it can be extended [S1].
+Dynamic maps. Choose for time-varying scenes. [7360-dynamic-mlp-maps.md]
 
 ### Neural Voxel Fields
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh uses sparse volumes, which are a form of voxel fields, but combines them with mesh [S1].
+Voxel-based neural fields. Choose for explicit volumetric control. [7824-neural-voxel-fields.md]
 
 ### Viewshed Fields (VF) Learning
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh differs in its hybrid approach [S1].
+View-dependent fields. Choose for occlusion handling. [7849-viewshed-fields-vf-learning.md]
 
 ### Primitive-bound feature embedding
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh uses RefBasis for view-dependent color modeling [S1].
+Embeds features per primitive. Choose for modular editing. [9161-primitive-bound-feature-embedding.md]
 
 ### Masked Wavelet Representation
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh differs in its representation and rendering [S1].
+Wavelet-based. Choose for multi-resolution detail. [9620-masked-wavelet-representation.md]
 
 ### Marching Neurons
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh uses marching cubes-like extraction from SDF, similar to Marching Neurons, but adds volume rendering [S1].
+Marching cubes on neurons. Choose for explicit mesh extraction. [9793-marching-neurons.md]
 
 ### Neural Network 3D Object Representation
-A sibling method [G]. VMesh is a specific instance of this, focusing on hybrid mesh-volume [S1].
+General NN 3D rep. Choose for standard implicit modeling. [11278-neural-network-3d-object-representation.md]
+
+### Neural Graph Mapping
+Graph-based mapping. Choose for topological structures. [11959-neural-graph-mapping.md]
+
+### Monte Carlo Volumetric Estimation
+MC estimation. Choose for unbiased rendering. [12926-monte-carlo-volumetric-estimation.md]
+
+### Patch-Grid
+Patch-based grid. Choose for local detail. [14449-patch-grid.md]
 
 ## Operating characteristics
